    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70400-8",
    "abstract": "The delivery of China's climate policy has substantial global implications, yet persistent gaps between policy targets and implementation raise concerns about policy credibility. This study presents a structured credibility assessment of 292 targets across 58 national climate and energy policies, including China's updated 2035 NDC. We apply a morphological scenario framework to examine interactions between policy uncertainty and socio-economic and technological drivers, embedding these scenarios in an integrated assessment model under two global contexts: one aligned with current NDCs and one consistent with global net-zero ambition. We find that timely or accelerated delivery of China's net-zero target could partially offset insufficient ambition elsewhere by mid-century. Full delivery by 2050 reduces global CO\u2082 emissions to 13 Gt, compared with 23 Gt without such policies. Policy delivery uncertainty alone could imply up to ~500 GtCO\u2082 difference in cumulative emissions by 2100 (~0.17 \u00b0C warming).",
    "field": "Climate change policy",
    "problem": "Persistent gaps between China's climate policy targets and actual implementation raise concerns about policy credibility, a critical uncertainty that is weakly defined and rarely quantified in a systematic way, especially in global climate models which often lack country-specific diversity.",
    "method": "This study presents a framework combining a structured credibility assessment of China's climate policies with morphological scenarios embedded in an integrated assessment model to quantify the global implications of policy delivery uncertainty.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the global implications of uncertainty in China's climate policy delivery by creating policy credibility-based scenarios and embedding them in an integrated assessment model to project emissions and warming.",
